WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.300
…testing those open access principles,

1b
00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:04.980
the integration of artificial intelligence
in automated

2
00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:08.360
content generation. The current rule is
absolute.

3
00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:12.260
While AI tools can instantly generate
complex text, write code,

4
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:16.360
and run predictive models, they possess
zero ethical or legal standing as

5
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.420
authors. Leading legal frameworks,
including the US Copyright Office,

6
00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.520
explicitly reject any claims of non-human
authorship.

7
00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:28.160
This rejection forces the scientific
community to adopt complex rules around

8
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.540
hybrid authorship to address human-AI
collaboration.

9
00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:35.400
Under these rules, a human researcher must
provide substantial,

10
00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.460
provable, and creative input to legitimately
claim credit for any AI-assisted

11
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.700
work. The underlying risks of bypassing
these rules are severe.

12
00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:48.400
Unchecked AI introduces hidden algorithmic
bias, causes critical replication

13
00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:50.660
failures, and a lack of explainability.

14
00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:54.160
Artificial intelligence drastically alters
how research is generated,

15
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:58.640
but the ultimate accountability for
errors, bias, and factual accuracy must

16
00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.084
remain entirely human.
